[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-BEHAVIOUR-MODEL
TITLE: Define the behaviour model in Nix and render it for review
DEPTH: interface-settled
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Define, as a typed Nix option tree layered over the generated grammar surface, what a
state, a move, a trigger, a gate and a flow ARE for any sdoc model, and render one
evaluation of it into artifacts a person can read.

WHERE IT LIVES. A new package, packages/strictdoc-behaviour, beside
packages/strictdoc-grammar rather than inside it: general purpose, intended for
publication, carrying the option types, a hand-written DSL over them, the emitters and
a foreign fixture. This repository's own behaviour values are a consumer file beside
the package, exactly the relationship values.nix has to the grammar surface. A devenv
module declares ai.strictdoc.behaviours.<name>, named for the tool the way
ai.strictdoc.grammars already is, with a grammar attribute naming a key in
ai.strictdoc.grammars and target paths for the emitted artifacts. Both modules declare
into one namespace and devenv.nix imports both.

HOW IT DIVERGES FROM MILESTONE ONE, and why that is the reason it is a separate
milestone. The grammar surface is GENERATED because strictdoc owns the definition of
what a .sgra file may contain, and a hand-written one would not notice an upstream
addition. Nothing upstream defines a behaviour model: strictdoc has no lifecycle, no
transition, no gate and no checkpoint. So there is no extractor here, no faithful
layer, no normalized layer and no surface-currency check. The option tree is
hand-written, and its only external constraint is the grammar it is layered over.

What DOES transfer is the discipline. One typed surface with a hand-written DSL that is
sugar and cannot weaken it. lib.types.attrTag for the genuine unions -- trigger kind,
subject kind, relation kind -- so an illegal combination is untypeable rather than
asserted. Every cross-entity rule in one apply per module that deepSeq forces the merged
value, resolves every id, cross-checks against the grammar, collects all errors and
throws once, because addCheck is silently never called on a submodule and a nested guard
does not run. The consumer value declared with the option TYPE at the module seam, since
a DSL asserts nothing by itself and a consumer handing raw values to an emitter passes
through no check at all. Generated artifacts committed, with a check that regenerates
and diffs.

One shape is deliberately inverted. The grammar surface uses LISTS because strictdoc
enforces element, field and relation order. The behaviour model has no order to
preserve, and list options merge by concatenation, which makes a list the wrong carrier
for anything a consumer overrides or specialises. Every behaviour entity is keyed by
attribute name and referenced by that name.

THE GRAMMAR IS AN INPUT, NOT A COPY. The behaviour surface consumes the CHECKED grammar
value -- the total one that comes back from the grammar package's own check step, with
every optional key present -- and derives its vocabulary from it: the element tags, the
fields each element carries, each single-choice field's option set, and the relation
roles. A state naming a value the grammar does not declare, a state field the element
does not carry, an element that does not exist, or a role nothing declares is an
evaluation error naming the vocabulary the grammar does declare. Nothing about the
grammar is retyped here, and this cross-check is why milestone three sits after
milestone one rather than beside it.

WHAT THE SURFACE DECLARES. Actors. Commands, which are requests and may be refused.
Events, which are accepted facts, including external ones such as landing in main.
Lifecycles, each with a subject, its state fields, its initial and terminal states, and
its transitions. Transitions, each with a trigger, a from and a to, its gates, what it
writes and what it emits. Operations, which mutate without moving a state field.
Gates and invariants, each declaring what it must SEE. Milestones, which are achieved
facts that can go stale. Checkpoints, each a record of when it fires and what it can
see. Validators, each naming a checkpoint and its status. Flows. Relation contracts:
which node types a role may point at, whether the relation admits cycles, and whether a
change propagates across it.

A subject is an element tag OR a relation role. That costs one union arm and keeps the
per-edge signing machine -- one PARENT_FP entry moving between absent, never signed,
signed and drifted -- expressible without anyone having to write one. Which carrier this
instance uses, a machine on the edge or a milestone on one end, is milestone five's.

Gate placement is DERIVED and never declared: a gate may run at every checkpoint whose
sees covers its needs. An empty run set is REPORTED, not fatal. That is measured rather
than cautious: the authorship guard needs both a git base ref and the acting identity,
no seam sees both, and four independent representations derive an empty set for it
today, so a strict mode would fail this repository's own model on day one. Corpus first,
gate second. Whether an empty run set ever becomes fatal is milestone four's.

Three eval-time checks the surface owes, all measured absent from every prototype and
measured doable in 0.05 s: a state no transition can reach, a lifecycle with no initial
or no terminal state, and two transitions firing on one trigger.

THREE SHAPES SETTLED HERE, each against a measured alternative, each cheap to flip
afterwards because flipping is a type edit plus a regenerate.

State is a TUPLE over the element's declared state fields, with initial, terminal, and
the rule that a transition may not write a field it does not constrain in from. The
alternative was one lifecycle region per single-choice field, where a transition moves
one region and a second region moves only on a completion trigger. Both are proven at
instance scale, 13 of 13 negatives in 0.04 s and 7 of 7 in 0.038 s. The tuple wins on
the defects: the region shape turns a spike's single atomic write into a completion
chain whose intermediate snapshot its own invariant forbids, has no transaction
primitive at all, and is violated today by a live unrun-and-sketch node in this corpus.
The tuple's two measured defects -- no reachability, and a sketch-to-verified leap
accepted -- are two of the three eval checks named above.

The ripple is DERIVED from what a transition writes intersected with what a gate reads,
at the granularity the field catalogue already carries: a per-field contract flag. The
alternative was a typed selector AST. Both derive the same invalidation edges; the flag
version exists, runs in 0.022 s and rejected both injected lies, while the AST is the
single largest unmeasured claim the prototype trees lean on, with neither its
tractability nor its over-approximation ever measured. WHICH fields are contract stays
empty here.

A flow is an ORDERED LIST OF QUALIFIED TRANSITION REFERENCES, each carrying only what
the transition does not already say: the expected outcome, taken or refused by a named
gate. It never restates a from, a to or a gate list. Measured against the alternative
tagged-step union: 25 of 34 authored steps restated their referenced transition, and a
transition edit produced a fresh "this flow never expects gate X" gap, so restatement
drifts by construction. Measured for the outcome field: without it no flow can express
a refusal, and 8 of 12 gates have no rejected path. Nothing executes a flow -- the tool
executes transitions, one verb to one transition -- so this is the cheapest of the three
to be wrong about. Optional, parallel, choose, loop, wait and compensation stay out
until an instance flow needs one, and if recursion ever returns it must pass through a
submodule, because a self-recursive listOf inside an attrTag infinite-recurses at option
evaluation.

WHAT ONE EVALUATION EMITS. A neutral JSON model carrying a schema version, a model
version and the resolved model -- the interchange form milestone four consumes, and
necessary rather than convenient because Python cannot evaluate Nix. Then, read from
that JSON and never from the Nix: a statechart per element as mermaid, a sequence
diagram per flow, a transition against gate matrix, a gate against checkpoint matrix
carrying the derived run sets with empty rows marked, a change-impact list, and a report
of what is still only prose -- a gate with no validator, a validator marked planned, a
transition no flow covers, an event no consumer reads. Every view is presentation and
none is authoritative.

The views are committed markdown with mermaid fences under the package's docs, written
by a devenv task in the same idempotent temp-file-and-rename shape as generate:sgra, and
invoked separately from anything that regenerates its inputs. They do not go inside
strictdoc's own site: mermaid renders on its markdown backend only, and this corpus does
not survive the default HTML export at all.

WHAT STAYS EMPTY. No states, no transitions, no gates, no actors, no commands, no
events, no flows, no checkpoints and no validator list. All of those are instance data.
The values shipped with this milestone are a TRANSCRIPTION of what this corpus does
today, taken from the spike's measurement and carrying no endorsement of it: they exist
so the diagrams have something to render and the negative fixtures something to reject.
Milestone five replaces them, and it is where the rules themselves get written.

Consumer surface the operator reviews before implementation: the option tree, and this
repository's transcription rendered through it. Reviewed after: every view regenerating
from one evaluation; a transition edit updating exactly the views that depend on it and
no others; a state value the grammar does not declare rejected at evaluation with the
grammar's own vocabulary in the diagnostic; and the gate against checkpoint table
showing the authorship guard with an empty run set.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator's stated reason for the whole roadmap is that the semantics cannot be
reasoned about without a DSL to see them. That is why this milestone delivers a surface
and its rendering together rather than in sequence: the rendering is not documentation
of the model, it is the reviewable form of it.

It is also why the three shape questions are settled here rather than brought to the
operator one at a time before any surface exists. Bringing them separately asks for a
prose judgement about prose, which is the exact failure mode the roadmap was opened to
end. Each of the three has two answers that were built and measured, each answer has a
measured defect, and the call is recorded with the losing argument attached -- so the
operator argues with a rendered model of their own graph instead of with a menu, and
flipping any one of the three afterwards costs a type edit and a regenerate.

Every part of this was prototyped in the spike and its review: both state shapes, both
flow shapes, both ripple mechanisms, four independent representations of gate placement
that derive the same result including that the authorship guard can run nowhere today,
and eight rendered views from one 0.055 s evaluation.

Generation is not the transferable part of milestone one. That surface is generated
because strictdoc owns the grammar and ships roughly three releases a month, so drift is
the risk. No upstream owns a behaviour model, so there is nothing to extract and nothing
to drift against; what transfers is the layering discipline, the union types, the single
forcing apply, and the module seam that makes the types unavoidable.
<<<
